1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,040
If just one civilisation had appeared in the Milky Way a million years before us…

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,327
we should see its traces everywhere today.

3
00:00:09,497 --> 00:00:11,602
But we see nothing.

4
00:00:11,772 --> 00:00:13,166
No confirmed message.

5
00:00:13,221 --> 00:00:14,394
No confirmed visit.

6
00:00:15,035 --> 00:00:17,449
No giant structure around a distant star.

7
00:00:18,304 --> 00:00:19,359
Only silence.

8
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:25,919
Çizgi has been holding this radio up for three hours.

9
00:00:26,023 --> 00:00:27,023
Nothing.

10
00:00:27,099 --> 00:00:28,353
Not even an advert.

11
00:00:29,253 --> 00:00:32,853
So if the universe is this big… why does it look so empty?

12
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:36,054
Maybe aliens are very rare.

13
00:00:36,376 --> 00:00:37,879
Maybe they're watching us.

14
00:00:37,988 --> 00:00:39,371
Maybe everyone is hiding.

15
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:45,077
Or maybe every civilisation, once it's advanced enough, hits the same wall.

16
00:00:45,899 --> 00:00:47,119
And if that's true… is that wall behind us?

17
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,387
Or still ahead?

18
00:00:52,194 --> 00:00:57,107
By the end, you'll see why that silence might be the scariest clue we have.

19
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:04,275
The Milky Way is about a hundred thousand light-years across,

20
00:01:04,315 --> 00:01:06,599
with one to four hundred billion stars.

21
00:01:07,248 --> 00:01:09,915
Count one star per second, without sleeping,

22
00:01:09,955 --> 00:01:13,342
and you'd still be counting for thousands of years.

23
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:15,590
And planets aren't rare.

24
00:01:15,943 --> 00:01:18,781
NASA has confirmed more than six thousand of them,

25
00:01:18,821 --> 00:01:21,776
and those are just the ones we've found so far.

26
00:01:22,612 --> 00:01:23,690
Some are molten.

27
00:01:23,730 --> 00:01:25,038
Some are gas giants.

28
00:01:25,371 --> 00:01:30,077
And some are roughly Earth-sized, in their star's habitable zone.

29
00:01:30,921 --> 00:01:36,624
But careful: habitable zone only means liquid water could exist there.

30
00:01:37,065 --> 00:01:38,358
Not that anyone does.

31
00:01:39,308 --> 00:01:44,287
A house on the right street doesn't prove someone lives inside.

32
00:01:44,637 --> 00:01:47,871
Still… hundreds of billions of stars.

33
00:01:48,081 --> 00:01:50,234
Billions of planets.

34
00:01:50,421 --> 00:01:52,419
Billions of years.

35
00:01:53,339 --> 00:01:58,794
With that many rolls of the dice… why do we only ever see ourselves?

36
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:05,164
It started at lunch.

37
00:02:05,490 --> 00:02:06,529
Summer, 1950, Los Alamos.

38
00:02:07,314 --> 00:02:10,802
Enrico Fermi and his colleagues were laughing about a newspaper

39
00:02:10,842 --> 00:02:14,900
cartoon that blamed missing New York rubbish bins on flying saucers.

40
00:02:15,936 --> 00:02:19,670
Aliens cross the galaxy… to steal bins.

41
00:02:20,241 --> 00:02:22,080
Scientists found this very funny.

42
00:02:23,227 --> 00:02:28,009
Then, halfway through the meal, Fermi suddenly asked: "But where is everybody?"

43
00:02:29,691 --> 00:02:31,120
Nobody laughed after that.

44
00:02:32,058 --> 00:02:39,789
Because a civilisation wouldn't need to fly near light speed to fill the galaxy.

45
00:02:40,243 --> 00:02:41,490
It sends a colony to a nearby star.

46
00:02:41,801 --> 00:02:42,801
That colony sends new ones.

47
00:02:43,085 --> 00:02:45,166
Wave after wave.

48
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:51,827
Even cautious estimates say that takes a few tens of millions of years.

49
00:02:52,385 --> 00:02:53,556
Long for us.

50
00:02:54,197 --> 00:02:57,687
But the galaxy is billions of years old.

51
00:02:58,892 --> 00:03:04,466
So if even one civilisation started long before us… where are its traces?

52
00:03:05,477 --> 00:03:07,094
We might not see their ships.

53
00:03:07,684 --> 00:03:10,049
But we could see their radio signals,

54
00:03:10,089 --> 00:03:14,954
the gases from their industry, or giant structures dimming their stars.

55
00:03:16,058 --> 00:03:19,041
Scientists call these technosignatures.

56
00:03:19,682 --> 00:03:22,298
So far, we haven't confirmed a single one.

57
00:03:23,246 --> 00:03:25,073
That doesn't prove nobody is there.

58
00:03:25,344 --> 00:03:29,372
Maybe we're looking in the wrong place, at the wrong time, the wrong way.

59
00:03:32,367 --> 00:03:37,921
In 1961, astronomer Frank Drake turned this into a list of smaller questions.

60
00:03:38,716 --> 00:03:40,369
How many stars are born?

61
00:03:40,497 --> 00:03:41,928
How many have planets?

62
00:03:42,136 --> 00:03:44,813
How many of those planets suit life?

63
00:03:45,589 --> 00:03:47,322
On how many does life start?

64
00:03:47,828 --> 00:03:49,382
How many reach intelligence?

65
00:03:49,967 --> 00:03:51,488
How many build technology?

66
00:03:52,539 --> 00:03:56,142
And the big one: how long does a civilisation stay detectable?

67
00:03:56,799 --> 00:03:59,150
The first answers we now know well.

68
00:03:59,675 --> 00:04:01,302
Planets are everywhere.

69
00:04:02,183 --> 00:04:09,287
But for life, intelligence and how long civilisations last, we have exactly one example.

70
00:04:10,232 --> 00:04:11,232
Us.

71
00:04:12,003 --> 00:04:16,256
It's like guessing every lottery ticket's odds by looking at a single winner.

72
00:04:17,151 --> 00:04:20,498
And honestly, the winner isn't sure how it won either.

73
00:04:21,513 --> 00:04:23,506
So depending on your guesses,

74
00:04:23,546 --> 00:04:28,984
the answer is anything from "we're alone" to "thousands of civilisations".

75
00:04:29,952 --> 00:04:34,439
And if the high numbers are right… how do we explain the silence?

76
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:41,736
The simplest answer: maybe life is incredibly hard to start.

77
00:04:43,615 --> 00:04:46,406
The right distance from a star may not be enough.

78
00:04:46,901 --> 00:04:52,550
You may also need the right atmosphere, the right chemistry, and billions of calm years.

79
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:57,497
Or simple life is common, but complex life is not.

80
00:04:58,278 --> 00:05:04,000
On Earth, life stayed microscopic for about three billion years.

81
00:05:04,967 --> 00:05:06,701
If Earth's history were one day,

82
00:05:06,741 --> 00:05:10,373
humans would show up in the last few seconds before midnight.

83
00:05:11,554 --> 00:05:16,164
The galaxy could be full of bacteria… and nobody to talk to.

84
00:05:17,352 --> 00:05:20,140
Maybe we're just one of the first to come this far.

85
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:24,703
Or maybe the problem is us.

86
00:05:25,394 --> 00:05:27,743
We've been sending radio for about a hundred years.

87
00:05:28,196 --> 00:05:29,635
Against the age of the galaxy,

88
00:05:29,675 --> 00:05:33,622
that's striking a match in a dark stadium and blowing it out straight away.

89
00:05:34,904 --> 00:05:39,128
To hear someone, we have to exist at the same time, look the right way,

90
00:05:39,168 --> 00:05:43,682
listen on the right frequency, and recognise a message when we get one.

91
00:05:44,678 --> 00:05:48,939
In August 1977, a telescope in Ohio caught a signal so strong

92
00:05:48,979 --> 00:05:53,812
that the astronomer on duty circled it and wrote one word: "Wow!"

93
00:05:55,152 --> 00:05:59,038
It lasted seventy-two seconds.

94
00:05:59,615 --> 00:06:03,079
We've listened to that spot for almost fifty years.

95
00:06:03,614 --> 00:06:04,954
It never came back.

96
00:06:06,144 --> 00:06:08,793
Maybe they talk in ways we can't detect.

97
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:12,494
An ant can't notice the Wi-Fi above its head.

98
00:06:13,382 --> 00:06:16,600
And distance makes every conversation slow.

99
00:06:17,304 --> 00:06:21,166
Say "hello" to someone a hundred light-years away,

100
00:06:21,206 --> 00:06:24,813
and the reply arrives in two hundred years.

101
00:06:25,363 --> 00:06:30,194
Speaking of silence: please don't let this comment section be as quiet as the universe.

102
00:06:30,550 --> 00:06:36,332
If you're enjoying this, a like and a subscribe really help this small channel.

103
00:06:37,183 --> 00:06:38,183
Now… the wall.

104
00:06:40,933 --> 00:06:43,453
The most unsettling idea is the Great Filter.

105
00:06:44,642 --> 00:06:49,691
Somewhere on the road from dead chemistry to a galaxy-spanning civilisation,

106
00:06:49,731 --> 00:06:53,196
there may be one step that almost nobody passes.

107
00:06:54,858 --> 00:06:56,429
Maybe it's the start of life.

108
00:06:56,626 --> 00:06:58,084
Maybe complex cells.

109
00:06:58,335 --> 00:06:59,484
Maybe intelligence.

110
00:07:00,373 --> 00:07:02,981
If the filter is behind us, that's good news.

111
00:07:03,430 --> 00:07:05,143
We already passed the hardest step.

112
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:10,394
But if it's ahead of us… the silence means something else.

113
00:07:11,812 --> 00:07:15,526
Maybe civilisations destroy themselves once they get powerful enough.

114
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:16,822
War.

115
00:07:16,862 --> 00:07:18,468
Uncontrolled technology.

116
00:07:18,736 --> 00:07:19,736
Collapse.

117
00:07:20,068 --> 00:07:23,164
Or something we don't have a name for yet.

118
00:07:23,334 --> 00:07:27,168
Maybe intelligence makes a species strong enough to reach the stars…

119
00:07:27,208 --> 00:07:30,022
but not wise enough to survive that strength.

120
00:07:31,273 --> 00:07:34,939
That's why the philosopher Nick Bostrom once wrote

121
00:07:34,979 --> 00:07:38,417
that he hopes our Mars probes find nothing.

122
00:07:38,952 --> 00:07:40,179
Not even a fossil.

123
00:07:41,430 --> 00:07:43,227
The Great Filter isn't proven.

124
00:07:43,598 --> 00:07:49,050
But the question it asks is real: is our power growing faster than our wisdom?

125
00:07:52,033 --> 00:07:57,909
The Zoo Hypothesis says advanced civilisations know we're here, and choose not to interfere.

126
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:01,308
Like us watching animals in a nature reserve.

127
00:08:02,093 --> 00:08:04,726
Which would mean, somewhere out there,

128
00:08:04,766 --> 00:08:08,533
someone is watching us try to fold a fitted sheet.

129
00:08:09,457 --> 00:08:10,769
There's no evidence for it.

130
00:08:11,141 --> 00:08:15,377
And every civilisation would have to keep the same secret.

131
00:08:15,547 --> 00:08:19,130
Darker still is the Dark Forest: everyone hides,

132
00:08:19,170 --> 00:08:23,269
because no one can be sure the others are friendly.

133
00:08:24,326 --> 00:08:29,886
So the universe might be quiet not because it's empty… but because everyone is hiding.

134
00:08:31,013 --> 00:08:32,515
It's a frightening idea.

135
00:08:33,003 --> 00:08:34,290
But it's speculation.

136
00:08:35,058 --> 00:08:37,246
When we don't know the reason for a silence,

137
00:08:37,286 --> 00:08:39,409
we easily fill it with our own fears.

138
00:08:42,300 --> 00:08:44,858
Or maybe there's no paradox at all.

139
00:08:45,541 --> 00:08:50,979
In 2018, astronomers added up every search for alien signals ever done.

140
00:08:51,370 --> 00:08:53,154
Compared with what's out there,

141
00:08:53,194 --> 00:08:57,506
it was like checking one hot tub of water from all of Earth's oceans.

142
00:08:58,576 --> 00:09:02,701
Find no whale in that hot tub, and declare: "There are no whales."

143
00:09:04,087 --> 00:09:08,003
We also assume every advanced civilisation wants to spread.

144
00:09:08,646 --> 00:09:09,892
Maybe they stay home.

145
00:09:09,933 --> 00:09:11,204
Maybe they go digital.

146
00:09:11,665 --> 00:09:13,680
Maybe they get quieter, not louder.

147
00:09:14,668 --> 00:09:19,446
The real power of the Fermi Paradox is that it exposes our assumptions.

148
00:09:19,822 --> 00:09:21,727
That intelligence is inevitable.

149
00:09:22,088 --> 00:09:24,200
That civilisations last.

150
00:09:24,765 --> 00:09:26,662
That progress means expansion.

151
00:09:27,755 --> 00:09:29,335
Maybe none of that is true.

152
00:09:32,167 --> 00:09:33,700
Here's what we know today.

153
00:09:34,703 --> 00:09:36,951
Hundreds of billions of stars.

154
00:09:37,238 --> 00:09:38,890
Billions of planets.

155
00:09:39,257 --> 00:09:41,831
Worlds that might suit life.

156
00:09:42,672 --> 00:09:46,721
And still no confirmed sign of anyone else.

157
00:09:47,896 --> 00:09:53,213
Now imagine that tomorrow we find life under Mars, or in Europa's ocean.

158
00:09:54,115 --> 00:09:55,970
We'd know we're not alone.

159
00:09:56,224 --> 00:09:58,309
But a harder question would get louder:

160
00:09:59,112 --> 00:10:04,757
if the beginning is that easy… why can't we see anyone who reached the end?

161
00:10:05,932 --> 00:10:11,367
Maybe the silence isn't telling us about aliens… but about the future of civilisations.

162
00:10:11,766 --> 00:10:12,838
Including ours.

163
00:10:14,699 --> 00:10:17,870
If the universe is empty… we're alone.

164
00:10:19,461 --> 00:10:23,031
If it isn't… why is everyone so quiet?
